cff-version: 1.2.0
title: Interaction Transfer to Unseen Scientific Entities
message: >-
  If you use this software or its results, please cite it using the
  metadata below. Please also cite the underlying datasets, which have
  their own authors and licences and are listed in THIRD_PARTY_DATA.md.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Sarukkai
    given-names: Akshath
repository-code: https://github.com/akshathsarukkai/interaction-transfer
url: https://github.com/akshathsarukkai/interaction-transfer
abstract: >-
  Research code and committed evidence for studying when pair-specific
  interaction effects transfer from observed combinations to unseen
  scientific entities. The outcome of combining two entities is
  decomposed as y(i,j) = f(i) + f(j) + I(i,j), and the project asks when
  the pair term I generalises across three regimes of increasing
  difficulty: an unseen combination of seen entities, a combination
  involving one entity never observed, and a combination in which
  neither entity was observed. Evaluated on two external systems - the
  Koplev sequential anticancer drug screen, where the directional effect
  splits exactly into a generic ordering potential and a pair-specific
  cyclic component, and the ChemLex acid-amine coupling high-throughput
  screen, where the interaction is bipartite. Includes a falsification
  study against the estimator that produced the drug-screen target, four
  pre-registered decision rules published verbatim, and an explicit
  record of the claims that were withdrawn.
keywords:
  - scientific-machine-learning
  - molecular-machine-learning
  - drug-combinations
  - reaction-prediction
  - out-of-distribution
  - compositional-generalization
  - chemistry
  - machine-learning
